= Tribulus Terrestris (Gokṣura)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — gokṣura is a leading mūtravirajanīya (urine-clearing) and vājīkara drug of the saṃhitās, and one of the ten roots (daśamūla, in the laghu-pañcamūla). Chinese: fully classical — 白蒺藜 (Tribulus terrestris) is a Bencao drug, with the important warning that it is botanically and pharmacologically distinct from 沙苑子 (Astragalus complanatus), with which it is frequently confused. Greco-Roman: fully classical — Dioscorides treats τρίβολος for urinary stones and dysuria; Pliny continues the tradition. A rare instance of a drug with three independent classical traditions, and one in which the three converge on the urinary/genitourinary use with a striking cross-system divergence on the "androgenic / testosterone" claim (modern, weak)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Tribulus terrestris_ L., family Zygophyllaceae; an annual/biennial creeping herb with characteristic spiny fruits. The drug is the *fruit* (the spiny schizocarp, _Tribuli fructus_) and the *root* in some preparations. _Tribulus_ is direct from Greek τρίβολος (_tríbolos_), "three-pointed" — τρι- (_tri-_) + the root of βάλλω (_bállō_, "to throw") → -βολος ("thrown" or "throwing") — extended to mean a "caltrop" (a four-spiked weapon thrown on the ground to maim cavalry horses) and, by metaphor, the spiny fruit of the plant. _terrestris_ = "of the earth, ground-creeping" (Latin _terra_), of its prostrate habit.#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _τρίβολος_, _βάλλω_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_; Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _tribulus_, _terrester_). The "three-pointed" / caltrop reading is the standard derivation, with the plant named for the same shape as the weapon.]

*English:* _Tribulus_ — the genus name retained as English drug name; also *puncture vine*, *caltrops*, *goathead* (from the appearance of the spiny fruit on bare feet, hooves, and tires).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "caltrop, n.", "puncture-vine"); the "caltrop" word in English is a 14th-c. military term ultimately from Latin _calx_ (heel) + _trappa_ (trap), but the modern usage for Tribulus follows from the convergent shape; standard English usage.]

*Chinese:* The classical drug is 白蒺藜 (báijíli), "white tribulus" — distinguished from 沙苑子 (shāyuànzǐ, "Shayuan seed"), which is *Astragalus complanatus* R.Br., a different species in Fabaceae, *not* Tribulus. The two are sometimes lumped historically under 蒺藜 (jíli), and the conflation is the single most common species-identification error in TCM materia-medica. Sub-forms: 蒺藜子 (jílizǐ, fruit) is identical with 白蒺藜; 蒺藜根 (jílígēn, root) is less used. The Chinese drug is *clearly Tribulus terrestris* when 白蒺藜 is specified.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the 白蒺藜 / 沙苑子 distinction is critical and well documented — Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004) treats them as separate drugs with different functions and emphasises the conflation risk; the _Bencao Gangmu_ entries should be read carefully.]

*Sanskrit:* गोक्षुर (_gokṣura_), गोक्षुरक (_gokṣuraka_) — "cow-hoof" — गो (_go_, "cow") + क्षुर (_kṣura_, "hoof; razor"); the spiny fruit's shape is read as a hoof-imprint (the alternative "razor" gloss is less common). Synonyms: त्रिकण्टक (_trikaṇṭaka_, "three-thorned" — semantically identical to Greek _tríbolos_), स्वादुकण्टक (_svādukaṇṭaka_, "sweet-thorn"), क्षुरक, इक्षुगन्धा.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _gokṣura_, _kṣura_, _trikaṇṭaka_); synonyms from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_. The semantic convergence between Sanskrit _trikaṇṭaka_ and Greek _tríbolos_ ("three-thorn") on the same shape-naming logic is a clean cross-system parallel.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

白蒺藜 is a fully canonical Liver-channel drug, treated since the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (where it is in the superior class, 上品); the Chinese reading is principally *Liver* — wind-extinguishing, eye-brightening, and qi/blood-moving — *not* primarily a urinary or sexual drug. This is a real cross-system divergence with both Ayurvedic and Greco-Roman uses.#footnote[白蒺藜 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (神農本草經), superior class; full entry in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The Chinese-vs-Ayurvedic/Greco-Roman divergence on principal use is well documented and is one of the cleaner cross-system divergences in materia-medica comparative work.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平), slightly warm.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Bitter (苦), pungent (辛).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Liver (肝), Lung (肺) — *not* Kidney or Bladder. This channel attribution is the key to the divergence: the Chinese reading places the drug squarely in the Liver-eye-wind axis.#footnote[Property, flavor and channels: _Bencao Gangmu_ 白蒺藜 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The Liver-Lung (not Kidney) channel placement is canonical and is the basis of the Chinese clinical use.]

*Actions & indications:*
- 平肝疏肝 — calms Liver and releases Liver constraint; for headache, dizziness, irritability, hypertension from Liver-yang rising or Liver-qi stagnation.
- 祛風明目 — dispels wind and brightens the eyes; the principal classical Chinese use, for red, itchy, irritated eyes from wind-heat and Liver-channel disturbance; nyctalopia, screen / floaters in some readings.
- 行氣活血 — moves qi and invigorates blood.
- 散結 — dissolves nodules; classically used for breast nodules, breast distension with PMS, and mammary stagnation.
- 止癢 — stops itching; for wind-heat skin disorders, urticaria, vitiligo (modern application).
- *Note on the Ayurvedic / Greco-Roman urinary use:* Chinese medicine *does not* primarily classify 白蒺藜 as a urinary or genitourinary drug. The "tonify Kidney / promote sexual function" use commonly attributed to "tribulus" in modern Western marketing draws on the Ayurvedic and modern pharmacological literature, not on classical Chinese sources.#footnote[Action list: _Bencao Gangmu_ 白蒺藜 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The cross-system divergence note is a deliberate clarification — the popular Western "TCM tribulus = testosterone booster" claim mis-cites the Chinese tradition; the Chinese drug is Liver-eye-wind, not Kidney-yang. This is one of the cleaner cross-system divergence cases.]

*Cautions:* Pungent and slightly warming; classically contraindicated in blood-vacuity wind (true deficiency), pregnancy (the qi/blood-moving action), and in patients without genuine Liver-pattern signs.#footnote[Pregnancy and blood-vacuity cautions: standard Chinese pharmacy — see Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Gokṣura is a leading classical drug for the urinary system and male reproductive vigor — *the* canonical Ayurvedic herb for stones, dysuria, and vacuity-pattern male sexual depletion. It is included in Caraka's _mūtravirajanīya gaṇa_ (urine-clearing group) and is a member of the _laghu-pañcamūla_ ("five lesser roots"; gokṣura, bṛhatī, kaṇṭakārī, śālaparṇī, pṛśniparṇī) and therefore of the _daśamūla_ ("ten roots") foundational formula.#footnote[Gokṣura in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 4 (_mūtravirajanīya gaṇa_, _bṛṃhaṇīya gaṇa_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Dhanvantari Nighaṇṭu; Rāja Nighaṇṭu. _Laghu-pañcamūla_ / _daśamūla_ membership: standard Ayurvedic formulary; cf. _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ and _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I (Daśamūla kvātha).]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous).

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — gokṣura is classically *cooling*, not warming; this distinguishes it from the warming Chinese reading.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of gokṣura: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_. The sweet-cooling profile (vs. the Chinese bitter-pungent-neutral/warm) is the basis of the cross-system divergence on indication.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* *Aśmari-bhedana* — *lithotriptic* (stone-breaking) — is the classical prabhāva of gokṣura. The dissolution of urinary calculi (_aśmari_) is not derivable from the sweet-heavy-cooling-sweet-vipāka profile alone, and is universally cited at prabhāva level in the saṃhitās and nighaṇṭus. This is one of the cleaner classical prabhāva assignments in the materia medica.#footnote[Aśmari-bhedana as prabhāva of gokṣura: Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 26 (_trimarmīya cikitsā_, urinary disorders); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_; canonical and widely commented on as a classical example of named prabhāva. Cf. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _gokṣura_.]

*Doṣa effect:* Tridoṣa-pacifying (tridoṣa-śāmaka), particularly pacifying Vāta and Pitta; mildly Kapha-aggravating in excess.#footnote[Doṣa attribution: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_; standard dravyaguna. The tridoṣa-pacifying classification supports its near-universal applicability in urinary disorders.]

*Karma (actions):*
- _Mūtrala_ — diuretic; promotes urination.
- _Mūtrakṛcchra-hara_ — relieves _mūtrakṛcchra_ (dysuria, painful urination, urinary obstruction).
- _Aśmari-bhedana_ — lithotriptic; breaks urinary stones (prabhāva).
- _Vṛṣya_, _śukrala_, _vājīkara_ — aphrodisiac, sperm-promoting; principal classical male tonic alongside aśvagandhā, with a different doṣa profile (cooling vs. warming).
- _Balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_, _rasāyana_ — strengthening, nourishing, rejuvenative.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic.
- _Indicated for:_ _mūtrakṛcchra_ (dysuria), _aśmari_ (calculi), _mūtraghāta_ (urinary retention), _prameha_ (urinary disorders / diabetic-spectrum), _klaibya_ (impotence), _kṣīṇa-śukra_ (sperm depletion), _basti-roga_ (bladder disorders), _vātavyādhi_ (in compound, via _daśamūla_), and as a general bṛṃhaṇa for genitourinary debility.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 26 (urinary disorders), Sūtrasthāna 4 (gaṇa lists); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Uttaratantra (urinary chapters); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Guḍūcyādi-varga_. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka_; Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:* slightly Kapha-aggravating; use cautiously in Kapha excess and in conditions with heavy oedema (_śotha_) of Kapha origin.
- *(—) Named saṃyoga viruddhas:* few in the classical record — gokṣura is more often a compound-formula ingredient (daśamūla) than a substance with named antagonists.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Pregnancy:* avoid — based on classical caution (qi/blood-moving Chinese reading) and limited modern safety data; some animal studies show reproductive-toxicity concerns at high doses.
  - *Diuretics / electrolyte concerns:* additive diuretic effect; monitor potassium.
  - *Anticoagulants / antiplatelets:* theoretical bleeding-time effect; clinical evidence sparse but flagged.
  - *Hormonal therapy:* the popular "testosterone-boosting" claim is *weak* — most rigorous trials (especially in eugonadal men) show *no significant* testosterone effect; some small studies show modest improvement in libido without testosterone change. Patients on hormone therapy / monitoring should be aware of marketing claims vs. evidence.
  - *Hepatotoxicity (rare):* a small number of cases reported with proprietary tribulus extracts; rare but documented.#footnote[The "testosterone-booster" claim: substantially overstated in popular literature; rigorous meta-analyses (cf. systematic reviews in _Andrology_ and related journals) show no consistent significant testosterone-raising effect in eugonadal men. Libido effects (without hormone change) are reported more consistently but with modest effect sizes — verify against current systematic reviews. The pregnancy caution is the conservative position; hepatotoxicity is rare. All (M) cautions are explicitly flagged as modern.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Tribulus is fully canonical in Tibb — the Tibb-Ayurvedic-Greco-Roman tri-system convergence on urinary stones reaches its medieval Arabic peak in Avicenna's treatment. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* قطب (_quṭb_) — the canonical Avicennan name; حسك (_ḥasak_) — Levantine usage. Persian: خار خسک (_khār-khasak_, "spiny-tribulus"). Urdu/Hakeemi: گوکھرو (_gokharū_, Hindi-derived); خار خسک (_khār-khasak_).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _quṭb_; with some Hot-Dry readings in other sources). The cooling-drying classification converges with the Ayurvedic śīta vīrya — clean classical convergence on the urinary stone-breaking drug across three independent traditions.

*Af'āl:* *Mufattit li-l-ḥaṣā* (stone-breaking — the canonical Avicennan attribution, paralleling exactly the Ayurvedic _aśmari-bhedana_); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *muqawwī al-kalā* (kidney-tonic); *muqawwī al-bāh* (aphrodisiac, especially in Indian Unani); *muḥsin li-l-mathāna* (bladder-improving); *muqawwī al-jasad* (in compound).

*Istemālāt:* renal and bladder stones (the principal indication, with classical tri-system convergence); dysuria; urinary obstruction; male sexual debility (Mughal-Hakeemi indication, paralleling the Ayurvedic vājīkara); chronic urinary inflammation.

*Mawāne':* pregnancy at therapeutic dose; severe debility.

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; rose-water; almond.

*Badal:* fennel; parsley (*baqdūnis*) for diuretic; other lithotriptic seeds.

*Tibb formulas:* *Joshānda-yi Khār-khasak* (tribulus decoction — the classical lithotriptic preparation); *Ḥabb-i Khār-khasak*; *Maʿjūn-i Khār-khasak* (especially in Mughal Unani aphrodisiac compound, paralleling Ayurvedic Gokṣurādi guggulu); *Sharbat-i Bazūrī* may include tribulus among the diuretic seeds.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _quṭb_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _ḥasak / quṭb_ entry — comprehensive Andalusian treatment; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _gokharū / khār-khasak_.#footnote[Tibb quṭb: Avicenna's lithotriptic attribution is the medieval Arabic-Persian preservation of the Dioscoridean λίθων κατάγμα reading; the tri-system convergence with the Ayurvedic aśmari-bhedana prabhāva is one of the cleanest in materia medica.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *白蒺藜 single-herb* — 6–10 g in decoction; for Liver-pattern eye and skin disorders, breast nodules, headache.
- *杞菊地黃丸 (Qǐ Jú Dìhuáng Wán)* tradition — Liver-Kidney yin-tonifying eye formulas; 蒺藜 sometimes added as a modification for active eye irritation.
- *當歸飲子 (Dāngguī Yǐnzǐ, "Tangkuei Decoction")* — for chronic itching with blood vacuity-wind; 蒺藜 in formula.
- *Topical / decoction for skin:* 蒺藜 + 防風 + 蟬蛻 (siler + cicada-shed) for wind-heat itching, urticaria, post-zoster eruption.
- *白蒺藜煎 (Báijíli Jiān)* — single-herb decoction for breast distension with PMS.
- *Modern functional / cardiovascular:* modern Chinese herbal cardiovascular practice uses 蒺藜 in compound for hypertension; modern, not classical.#footnote[Formula sources: _Bencao Gangmu_ 白蒺藜 entry; Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009); 當歸飲子 from _Yánnián Zhìbǎo Lùn_ (延年至寶論). The modern cardiovascular use is functional-medicine extension; flagged.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Gokṣura cūrṇa* — 3–6 g of the powdered fruit with warm milk or water; the foundational classical preparation for urinary complaints.
- *Gokṣura kvātha* — fruit decoction, 50–100 mL; for _mūtrakṛcchra_, _aśmari_, _prameha_.
- *Gokṣurādi guggulu (गोक्षुरादिगुग्गुलु)* — the principal compound formula for urinary stones and urinary-tract disease; gokṣura with guggulu, triphala, and warming/lithotriptic supporting drugs. A standing classical formula.
- *Daśamūla kvātha (दशमूलक्वाथ)* — the "ten roots decoction"; gokṣura is one of the _laghu-pañcamūla_ ("five lesser roots"). Used principally for Vāta disorders and as a postpartum tonic.
- *Daśamūlāriṣṭa (दशमूलारिष्ट)* — fermented decoction with the ten roots, including gokṣura; for postpartum recovery, Vāta-disease, debility.
- *Gokṣurādi cūrṇa* — compound powder including gokṣura, vidārī, śatāvarī, aśvagandhā, etc.; classical _vājīkara_ formula.
- *Trikaṇṭakādi kvātha* — alternative classical formula with gokṣura as chief.
- *Sukumāra ghṛta* — medicated ghee containing gokṣura among other drugs; women's/post-partum tonic.#footnote[Formula sources: _Bhaiṣajyaratnāvalī_ (Gokṣurādi guggulu); _Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ (Daśamūla kvātha, Daśamūlāriṣṭa); _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I (standard recipes). Translations: Murthy, _Sharangadhara_.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

This is the most striking cross-system feature: Tribulus has a continuous classical European tradition.

- *Greco-Roman:* _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ IV.15) treats τρίβολος in two distinct entries: *terrestrial tribulus* (τρίβολος χερσαῖος, _Tribulus terrestris_) and *aquatic tribulus* (τρίβολος ἔνυδρος, _Trapa natans_, the water chestnut — a genuinely different plant in Lythraceae, named for the same caltrop shape of the fruit). The terrestrial tribulus is prescribed for *kidney stones* (λίθων κατάγμα — "breaking of stones"), *dysuria*, and *gravel* — a precise convergence with the Ayurvedic _aśmari-bhedana_ classical use. _Pliny_ (_Naturalis Historia_ XXII) continues the tradition with similar uses. _Galen_ mentions it in the same register.
- *Galenic / medieval European:* tribulus carries forward in medieval herbalism as a urinary / lithotriptic drug; entries in the Renaissance herbals (Mattioli's commentary on Dioscorides; Gerard's _Herball_). It is *not* one of the major modern European pharmacopoeial drugs, but the urinary-stone use is documented continuously.
- *Modern Western:* the late-20th-c. revival of tribulus as a "testosterone booster" derives from Eastern-European (Bulgarian) sports-pharmacology / supplement marketing, *not* from the classical European tradition — which is firmly a urinary, not androgenic, drug. The Greco-Roman use converges with the Ayurvedic use (urinary + sexual depletion) — both have a male-vigor element — but the modern androgenic-supplement framing is post-classical marketing.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ IV.15 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann) — the two-entry treatment (land vs. water tribulus) is in the original; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XXII (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On Simple Drugs_ VII. The cross-system convergence on urinary/lithotriptic use across Dioscorides + gokṣura + (modern pharmacological) is real and well-attested. The Bulgarian-supplement-industry origin of the "tribulus = testosterone" framing (late 20th c.) is documented in the supplement-history literature and is honestly modern, not classical.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Tribulus is a medicinal, not a food nutrient source.

*Key constituents:*
- *Steroidal saponins* — the principal active class; major members *protodioscin*, *prototribestin*, *dioscin*, *gracillin*, *terrestrosin*, *tribulosin*. Standardized extracts are typically characterized by protodioscin or total saponin content.
- *Furostanol and spirostanol* saponin classes (the aglycones include _diosgenin_, _tigogenin_, _gitogenin_, _hecogenin_).
- *Flavonoids* (rutin, kaempferol, quercetin derivatives).
- *Alkaloids* — β-carbolines (harman, norharman) in trace amounts.
- *Sterols* (β-sitosterol, stigmasterol).#footnote[Phytochemistry of _Tribulus terrestris_: WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_, vol. 5, _Fructus Tribuli_; reviews in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; substantial variation in saponin content between Bulgarian, Indian, and Chinese growing regions and between fruit and root extracts — flagged for verification.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Diuretic and lithotriptic:* preclinical and small clinical studies support the classical _aśmari-bhedana_ / Dioscoridean use; modest evidence for stone passage and dysuria relief. The classical-modern convergence here is real.
- *Androgenic / testosterone:* the famous modern claim — and the substantially over-stated one. Rigorous systematic reviews and meta-analyses (cf. _Andrology_, _Journal of Sex Medicine_, and related) find *no consistent significant* testosterone-raising effect in eugonadal men. Some modest libido and erectile-function improvements without hormone change have been reported, plausibly via a nitric-oxide / smooth-muscle relaxant mechanism. Effect sizes are small and study quality variable.
- *Cardiovascular:* mild antihypertensive and antiplatelet effects in preclinical models; clinical evidence preliminary.
- *Cholesterol-lowering:* preclinical hypolipidemic activity; clinical evidence modest.
- *Anti-inflammatory / antioxidant:* standard for the saponin class; preclinical.
- *Plant-tops in livestock:* the *flowering and seed plant* in pastures can cause *photosensitization* and *hepatotoxicity* in sheep ("geeldikkop"); a genuine veterinary syndrome and a documented concern for grazing livestock, distinct from the human medicinal use of the fruit.
- *β-carboline alkaloids:* present in trace amounts; their MAO-inhibitory potential has been raised theoretically as an interaction concern, but clinical significance is unclear.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the lithotriptic + diuretic axis is well-supported and converges across Ayurveda + Greco-Roman + modern pharmacology. The "testosterone" claim is the *divergence point*: classical European and Chinese readings do not place tribulus in the androgenic register; the Ayurvedic vṛṣya/vājīkara register is sexual function broadly (libido, erectile, sperm), not specifically testosterone-elevation; modern marketing has conflated these.#footnote[Diuretic / lithotriptic clinical evidence: small RCTs and case series — verify against current systematic reviews in urology. Testosterone meta-analyses: cf. _Andrology_, _Journal of Sex Medicine_, _Maturitas_; the predominant conclusion is no significant testosterone effect in eugonadal men, modest libido / sexual-function improvement. Livestock photosensitization (geeldikkop): veterinary toxicology literature. The cross-system convergence / divergence reading is the author's synthesis and is the cleaner interpretation of the data.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India:* fruit powder, 3–6 g/day; decoction 50–100 mL.
- *Chinese pharmacy:* 白蒺藜 in decoction 6–10 g/day.
- *Standardized extracts (modern):* 250–1500 mg/day of fruit extract standardized to protodioscin or total saponins (40–60%); clinical-trial ranges 500–1500 mg/day for 4–12 weeks; long-term safety beyond ~3 months not well characterized.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) *pregnancy* — avoid (conservative); (b) concurrent *diuretic* therapy (additive); (c) concurrent *anticoagulant* / antiplatelet therapy (theoretical bleeding); (d) hormone-sensitive cancer (theoretical, weak data); (e) *severe renal impairment* (caution; the urinary action may not be appropriate); (f) known prior hepatotoxic reaction.
- *Marketing-vs-evidence caveat:* the popular "testosterone booster" claim is *not* well supported in eugonadal men. Patients should be helped to distinguish marketing from evidence.#footnote[Dose ranges: Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, Part I, vol. 1, _Gokṣura_ monograph; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (白蒺藜); WHO _Monographs_, vol. 5; current clinical-trial literature. These are reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice. The marketing-vs-evidence note is a deliberate honesty point per the project's "don't overstate modern health claims" rule.]

